l in all  thanks to putin against this very sacked  sad backdrop  we have seen as strengthened nato  and in terms of countries doing more to  stop putin and nato doing more to stop  putin in terms of embargoes do you think  the uk is doing enough is there anything  more you would like to see us doing  i think the uk has been at the very  forefront  of helping ukraine i also appreciate  the regular  updates on intelligence provided by the  uk  so i think  ukraine can really count the uk among  its closest friends and that may also be  necessary once the war is over we have  to ensure  that it will never happen again that  ukraine will be invaded  so ukraine will need security guarantees  one way or the other and i think the uk  as well as the u.s and other big nato  allies will play a crucial role in  providing  iron-clad security guarantees  to ukraine  how do you talk about the war being over  how do you think it will play out do you  see an end game  well i think the biggest risk right now  uh is a prolonged conflict  uh because putin and russia  are experts  in  prolonged simmering frozen conflicts for  decades they have uh occupied and the  eastern part of moldova since 2008 they  have occupied upcase and south of sita  in the state of georgia since 2014 they  have been in the crimea and the eastern  part of  the runway region so i think  putin's  goal might be  to continue to destabilize and weaken  ukraine through a prolonged  conflict i think this is currently the  biggest risk and that's why we have to  deliver all the weapons that ukraine  needs  uh we we have delivered some weapons  haven't we but also although we are not  delivering fighters um officially there  are some people who've gone in there  unofficially from the uk to fight and  two of those british soldiers we know  have been captured by by the russians  possibly  now facing the death penalty is there  any hope  for them that you can offer to their  families what what do you think the  international community could do here  obviously we should do all we can to put  pressure on  russia  but i'm not in a position to  to be too optimistic in that respect we  have seen how russia  violates  all international laws and  conventions  so i think the best  tool would be to continue  a maximum pressure  on  russia the only language  that putin understands is a language of  strength  force and unity that's why we should  continue to put a maximum of pressure on  russia through delivery of all the  weapons  and cutting off all financing of  russia  but is there not also the danger that  that if you do push back too hard um  that he then escalates it further  no on the contrary i think history has  taught us  that appeasement with  dictators does not lead to peace on the  contrary it leads to  war and  conflict  and i've noted that someone  has proposed that it  would be dangerous to humiliate  putin but i mean  setting the precedent  that an aggressor  can achieve their total gains  through  a peace deal after pursuing an  unjustified  war that would only encourage other  dictators as well it would set it would  send a very dangerous  signal so i think we have only one thing  to do to continue  helping the ukrainians to win this war  okay anderson rasmussen uh former  secretary general of nato and prime  minister former of denmark as well thank  you very much for joining  us now new data reveals that twice as  many online gambling accounts are linked  to people in deprived areas compared to  the most wealthy regions the report  commissioned by the charity gamble aware  says those players are also more likely  to suffer heavy losses and found that  the online gambling industry relies on a  small percentage of customers with the  largest losses for most of its revenue  we're joining me now aliz and charles  richie whose son jack took his own life  as a result of online gambling uh really  good to talk to you both thank you very  much for joining us this morning um just  tell us a little bit about jack and how  he got into gambling and what impact it  had  well jack started gambling while he was  at school  he and a large party of his friends used  to go out and gamble in their dinner  hour and actually jack became addicted  to the  land-based equivalent of these very  uh addictive games um  it was actually the fixed stop betting  terminals but the same same things are  available online he moved online very  quickly  and he was very seriously addicted and i  and what the coroner found that his  inquest which we had last march  um was that the information available to  him and to us was absolutely woeful the  coroner used that word woeful  uh and um  to be honest i mean we're as campaigners  and we met up with other bereaved  families very quickly we formed our  charity gambling with lives and we've  been campaigning for a long time  about these uh forms of gambling that  have been found to be very very  addictive online frankly it's been known  for a long time  and  we are pleased that finally the uh  gambling industry uh partner charities  have spoken out about this with this  research  but to be honest it's taken an awful  long time and people have died along the  way  the truth is anybody addicted to these  terrible terrible forms of gambling  and and how did the addiction in  gambling contribute to his death  uh well he was very clear in his so side  note and the uh that it was because of  gambling i mean he'd been free for  months and months before he died but  he will have been induced back by some  kind of email the coroner was very clear  about it and issued three prevention of  future death notices for three different  government departments  um to say that the government has to act  to stop this  charles the the impact of this  like like drugs i suppose when a child  gets involved with that it's  far-reaching isn't it what has the  impact been on your family  well i mean it's it's obviously  transformed our lives we um as liz said  very shortly after jack's death we we  set up gambling with lives  and met with other families who'd been  similarly bereaved um and so  the charity  does obviously family support mutual and  peer support with each other and helping  families newly bereaved  but but we're campaigning you know that  that that what we what we need is to  make sure that the lessons from all the  deaths that happen every year  and public health england estimate there  are over  400 gambling related suicides every year  in england alone and the lessons need to  be learned from those deaths and so  major reforms are needed around products  and this research points to the online  casino and slot games  those games need to be made much safer  less dangerous  we also need affordability checks and i  think that this  report  really highlights that that issue about  deprivation is that the industry relies  on the majority of its profits from a  very small number of accounts  and a third of those are  families in deprived areas so we have to  have affordability checks which kick in  at a level  which are meaningful i think there's  kind of an acceptance now that there  needs to be some form of affordability  checks but but people are talking about  that well it kicks in at 500 pounds  losses a month or more that is that  these figures show that that would be a  totally inadequate response um we've got  is it three percent of accounts losing  more than two thousand pounds a year on  slots  one percent of accounts uh accounting  for 40 percent of the losses and  the  we we we have to have action on on  affordability at uh preventative  uh levels and the point is that millions  of people in the country are affected by  this  most of them are in deprived areas but  actually the minister said very recently  anybody can be addicted to these very  addictive products they have higher  addiction at risk rates than heroin it  is crazy that they are so available and  so advertised  what advice would you give liz to to  anybody who's who knows family members  who you think are heading uh in the in  the  in the direction of addiction to  gambling  it's asking what are you gambling on  because we know that some of these  products are so dangerous  what we need is proper information for  the public  but the reality is that the information  has been controlled by the industry  partner charities like gamble aware who  are subject to a voluntary  system of funding they can't afford to  be too clear with the public  about this and frankly the lack of  information has cost lives and unless  the public are properly warned like  we've been warned about smoking for  example  you know a five-year-old knows the  smoking kills who knows that gambling  kills  it's important  liz i'm so sorry we're going to have to  cut you off just because we're running  out of time in the program but  thank you both so much and thank you for  talking to us about jack  thank you thank you well if you've been  affected by anything discussed in this  interview you can always contact the  samaritans on double one six one two  three or email joe samaritans.org  we've got lots more to come stay with us  after this break  [Music]
 

